d on, ensuring the final SELECT clause on the outermost query matches the original query\'s expected outcome. - Before: SELECT col1, transformation_function(col2) FROM table WHERE condition; - After: SELECT col1, transformation_function(col2) FROM (SELECT col1, col2 FROM table WHERE condition) AS subquery;"""\n\nRule Subset Sequences:\n### Rule Sequence 1: ["AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 2: ["FILTER_INTO_JOIN", "FILTER_REDUCE_EXPRESSIONS", "PROJECT_FILTER_TRANSPOSE"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 3: ["FILTER_INTO_JOIN"]\n\n### Rule Sequence 4: ["AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE", "SORT_PROJECT_TRANSPOSE", "PROJECT_FILTER_TRANSPOSE"]'}], 'response': 'Here is the reasoning and the arranged rule names:\n\nReasoning:\nThe given SQL query can be rewritten using various strategies. Query Rewrite 1 moves filter conditions from the WHERE clause to the ON clause of INNER JOIN operations, reducing dataset sizes before joining. Query Rewrite 2 eliminates unnecessary layers and transformations by applying GROUP BY and aggregate functions directly to the data source. Query Rewrite 3 simplifies conditions in the WHERE clause using search arguments (Sarg) based on known constraints and constants. Query Rewrites 4-8 involve rearranging queries, combining operations, pre-calculating results, and condensing result sets to improve performance by reducing workload, minimizing dataset sizes, and avoiding unnecessary processing steps.\n\nArranged rule names:\n```python\n["FILTER_INTO_JOIN", "AGGREGATE_PROJECT_MERGE", "FILTER_REDUCE_EXPRESSIONS", "SORT_PROJECT_TRANSPOSE", "PROJECT_FILTER_TRANSPOSE"]\n```\nThis sequence combines the rules to optimize the given SQL query most effectively.', 'time': 2.249387741088867}
